pronoun spellings embedded in longer words are usually negative instead.
The preceding word adjusts English amplitudes (notably 'have' and 'all').
There are weaker personal-pronoun responses, a split-'their' response, and
a negative response to runs of middle dots. Everything else stays near zero.
